dConfigCopy, a row-21 handoff with notes on seven owning tasks, 19 of 21 rows driven (rows 15 and 21 explicitly not driven), 25+1 hosttrust and 4+1 config plants killed with a neutral SURVIVED control and one documented converge-ignore survivor by the staged-marker backstop. These are producer claims requiring independent verification; in particular decide whether the shared-reader release-then-reacquire path leaves a window in which a second writer stages a new marker between the shared release and the exclusive acquisition, and whether the 500ms lock verification retry can admit a replaced inode. Verify the three CR4 findings against production entry points, RED-first evidence and your own isolated reproduction:\n4|F1 (surviving-process admission): port or rerun TestReviewerCrashBetweenConfigAndGeneration and TestReviewerRollbackCrashSurvivingReader on the exact candidate; the surviving LoadCoherent must refuse or converge, never admit old generation 3/4; confirm the pending joint-commit marker is a general admission barrier across WithSharedSnapshot/readSnapshotLocked, readCommitted, authorization boundaries and mutation transactions, that stale requests are still refused (never silently refreshed), and that TestApplyV4CrashConvergesGeneration now asserts convergence with fatal expectations instead of returning silently on Load/Configuration/Open/ReadSnapshot errors. Cover apply and rollback, surviving and reopened processes, marker cleanup/error ordering. Distinguish tested refusal from proven recovery.\n5|F2 (first-open lock replacement): rerun TestReviewerCrossProcessLockInitialization; with the child paused after the absence observation and the parent holding the lock, the child must end up on the same inode and must not return while the parent holds it; confirm initialization is atomic and non-replacing through production Open, that existing-lock contention is tested, and read the Windows initialization path for the same rule (report Windows execution honest